  "text": "More significantly, our study explores the impact and potential mechanism of low-dose BIBR1532 combined with radiotherapy on the TIME. BIBR1532 plus RT activates the cGAS/STING signalling pathway, whereas BIBR1532 alone does not elicit this response. This cGAS activation, in synergy with IR, augments its sensitivity to cytoplasmic dsDNA, possibly attributable to BIBR1532 affecting telomerase activity alone. Importantly, before telomeres are shortened to a critical length, there is typically a substantial time lag [38]. Nevertheless, ionizing radiation (IR) induces exogenous DNA damage, directly breaking chromosomal DNA and generating substantial DSBs. The non-homologous end joining (NHEJ) pathway then initiates a rapid repair effort, but this template-free mechanism may inadvertently produce structurally aberrant chromosomal fragments, which become improperly segregated and culminate in micronuclei formation [39, 40]. Micronuclei may rupture and release dsDNA into the cytoplasm, activating the sensor cGAS, which catalyses the synthesis of cyclic GMP-AMP (cGAMP) and triggers the innate immune response [41]. Notably, BIBR1532 intensifies IR-induced DNA damage, enhancing phosphorylation of STING, TBK1, and IRF3 proteins in both tumour cells and dendritic cells (DCs), which increases interferon-beta (IFN-\u03b2) expression, fostering DC maturation and inciting an anti-tumour immune response.",
